<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · sea</p><h1 class="enc-title">North Sea</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The North Sea is a marginal sea of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-ocean">Atlantic Ocean</a> between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scandinavia">Scandinavia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. Its storms are notoriously unpredictable. After the defeat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-armada">Spanish Armada</a> in 1588, North Sea storms wrecked many of its ships, demonstrating the limits of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-empire">Spanish Empire</a>'s naval design in northern waters.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/north-sea.jpg" alt="North Sea" width="320" height="419" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: MODIS Rapid Response Team, NASA/GSFC · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:NASA_NorthSea1_2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
